"position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"B7Ky4JIYT2"}],"key":"H3yjAe1510"}],"key":"Cpg4UyyaOv"},{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"IU","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"J0EC609zTW"}],"key":"Alsv5jNibm"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Seit der Geburt","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"OYCn0aC7BK"}],"key":"ETdobmRB0I"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"38 of 85","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"TeucOplnjR"}],"key":"VCd6hYtje0"},{"type":"tableCell","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Genau jetzt (26)","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"S7gZqkiegP"}],"key":"t1KgWOFaeT"}],"key":"Ls5I2qtbIW"}],"key":"RCWySvAzwt"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Twenty participants matched all six sections, 16 missed one, 18 two, 18 three and 13 four or five. The number of non-matching answers differed between conditions (Kruskal–Wallis H = 7.01, p = .030; matching answers for the STICSA trait section, None .52, Partial .33, Full .48) and increased with the OCI score (Spearman ρ = .33, p = .002), but not significantly with any other total (all p ≥ .085).","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"J8G3ZohWoT"}],"key":"Y1rNDIDq88"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The non-matching answers to the STICSA trait question are not only a matter of the missing “in general” option. Of the 34 participants who answered “Genau jetzt” before the trait section, 31 gave the same answer before the state section, and their trait items were more similar to their state items. The difference between the trait and the state total was 0.4 points in this group and 3.9 points in the group answering “Seit der Geburt”, and at the same state score it was 3.4 points smaller (linear regression with the state total, HC3, p = .014). The share of the 21 items answered identically on both scales was 0.79 against 0.66 (Mann–Whitney p = .003; with both totals as covariates, +0.08, p = .063). Internal consistency of the trait scale was similar in both groups (Cronbach’s α .85 and .89).","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"wBKGPjrQrT"}],"key":"KZ1RGeII1A"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"As a check of the consequences for the behavioural results, the participant-level associations of the STICSA scales with the selectivity at t+1 and with the share of transitions followed at t+1 (see ","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"lN6EPLSkr7"},{"type":"link","url":"/results/task-structure/strategies-and-durations","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responses to Events and Placement Duration","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"QvEqD4rH14"}],"urlSource":"../../results/task_structure/strategies_and_durations.md","dataUrl":"/results.task-structure.strategies-and-durations.json","internal":true,"protocol":"file","key":"JUeJZrWGCP"},{"type":"text","value":") were estimated separately for the participants answering “Seit der Geburt” before the trait section and for the others (linear regressions with condition, HC3, standardized). For the STICSA trait total and the selectivity at t+1, the association was −0.22 SD per SD (p = .012) in all participants, −0.05 (p = .67, n = 37) in the matching group and −0.36 (p = .010, n = 48) in the others (interaction p = .089). The STICSA state total showed the same pattern (−0.09, n = 36, and −0.33, n = 48; interaction p = .33), as did the share of transitions followed. The difference between the groups is therefore not specific to the trait scale and cannot be attributed to the misreading of the trait instruction; it may reflect other differences between the participants who chose the two answers, or chance, given the subgroup sizes.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"JZz1kN8U8B"}],"key":"Ce0zCmjc1d"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interval between questionnaire and lab session","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"QyiuQeodTV"}],"identifier":"interval-between-questionnaire-and-lab-session","label":"Interval between questionnaire and lab session","html_id":"interval-between-questionnaire-and-lab-session","implicit":true,"key":"EvADGscBL2"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The questionnaires were completed online before the lab session, a median of 6.9 days earlier (quartiles 3.9 and 10.8 days, range 0 to 41.5 days); 13 participants completed them more than 14 days before the session and one shortly after it. The interval was not associated with any questionnaire total (all |ρ| ≤ .14) or with condition (Kruskal–Wallis p = .63).","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"mwKrRicEbY"}],"key":"Grt8tjx81K"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Completion times, attention checks and uniform answers","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"Gb0KrsH6zf"}],"identifier":"completion-times-attention-checks-and-uniform-answers","label":"Completion times, attention checks and uniform answers","html_id":"completion-times-attention-checks-and-uniform-answers","implicit":true,"key":"LpUd2W1w1t"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The median completion time was 24 minutes (10 to 103). No participant took less than 1 s per item in any section; four participants took less than 2 s per item in one section. Six participants failed one of the seven attention checks (IU, ACE, EAT-26 and SIAS one each, ERS two), and none failed more than one; their other indices (completion time, answers to the time-window questions, uniform answers) were unremarkable. The ","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"OTP6jj0VlX"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ac_*","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"LI2P4Ez06c"},{"type":"text","value":" columns of the scoring are FALSE also for unanswered checks, as in the incomplete response of 15265, which did not fail any answered check. Identical answers to all items of a scale occurred only as “not at all” on the symptom scales (STICSA trait 2, STICSA state 3, OCI 1, PCL-5 3) and as “no” on all items of the Adverse Childhood Experiences questionnaire (ACE; 33 participants), which correspond to low symptom levels and to the absence of adverse experiences rather than to inattentive answering; no participant answered the SIAS, the Eating Attitudes Test (EAT-26), the IU or the Emotion Reactivity Scale (ERS) uniformly.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"q4Sg1YfiwS"}],"key":"GEHG4N44Dv"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"BDI-II answer format","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"ZKxfMEL3DE"}],"identifier":"bdi-ii-answer-format","label":"BDI-II answer format","html_id":"bdi-ii-answer-format","implicit":true,"key":"cSNZYdk0Jj"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The BDI-II items were presented with one checkbox per statement, so that several statements of an item could be ticked. Thirteen participants ticked more than one statement on at least one item (22 items in total). Until 14 November 2026, the scoring script took the first ticked statement, which is the lowest value, whereas the BDI-II instruction is to choose the highest statement when several apply. The scoring now takes the highest ticked statement (","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"KCrqHN7n4l"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"questionnaire_scoring_pipeline.R","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"cN8DXKy3QS"},{"type":"text","value":", ","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"iShBxryrVP"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"score_bdi_item_4","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"b4Nb3KwdNx"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"TjORhPmlJH"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"score_bdi_item_7","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"rpSThvmgPL"},{"type":"text","value":"; backup ","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"HAAi0fGk1z"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"questionnaire_scoring_pipeline.R.bak_2026-11-14","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"SeG4J2m9Ym"},{"type":"text","value":"). This raises the totals of these 13 participants by 1 to 7 points, the number of participants with a total of at least 14 from 20 to 22 (at least 20, 11 in both scorings), and the two scorings correlate with ρ = .985. For one participant, the previous scoring gave 0 on item 9 (suicidal thoughts) although a higher statement was also ticked; the participant is identified in ","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"Ek4IqnS27Q"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"quality_flags_participant.csv","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"d4tBaX3SMf"},{"type":"text","value":" (columns ","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"mjEf1mhpQS"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bdi_item09_lowest","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"OUotG2ORPb"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"PnDHBoiGbw"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bdi_item09_highest","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"bOFA2PIqvP"},{"type":"text","value":") for the procedure of the lab. The text of the second statement of item 17 (irritability) repeats the first (“Ich bin reizbarer als sonst”) instead of “viel reizbarer”, so that the two cannot be distinguished by the participants.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"r7pprOXUbf"}],"key":"nE20vI5Oow"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scoring check","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"E0dOzK2dpZ"}],"identifier":"scoring-check","label":"Scoring check","html_id":"scoring-check","implicit":true,"key":"h9c1OpEO6W"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"All totals and subscale scores of the R scoring (","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"yNZApc6JrB"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"questionnaire_scoring_pipeline.R","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"ipeRn6a2ng"},{"type":"text","value":") were recomputed independently from the item-level export in Python for all 172 responses, with the item assignments and keys of the published scales (STICSA somatic and cognitive items after Ree et al., 2008; reverse keying of SIAS items 5, 9 and 11 and of the EAT-26 item on trying new rich foods). All totals and subscale scores agreed exactly, apart from the BDI-II totals of the participants with several ticked statements before the fix described above. All answer labels in the export were covered by the recoding of the scoring. The subscale assignment of the IU (prospective and inhibitory) follows the IUS-12 and is not a published assignment for the German IU-18.","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"TR8gPLA6IP"}],"key":"Pl9BVYjIAT"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Item and scale properties","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"VPRMpgP56Q"}],"identifier":"item-and-scale-properties","label":"Item and scale properties","html_id":"item-and-scale-properties","implicit":true,"key":"d3W229ji2L"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Internal consistency was high for all symptom scales (Cronbach’s α: STICSA trait .88, STICSA state .87, BDI-II .91, OCI .83, PCL-5 .94, IU .89, SIAS .91, EAT-26 .88, ERS .94) and low for the ACE checklist (.50), which counts different kinds of events rather than indicators of one construct. In the SIAS, the three reverse-keyed items had lower item-rest correlations (.31, .25 and .48) than the other items (.31 to .75).","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"LhVmBVpbx5"}],"key":"WW1eMHMOKD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The totals cover a broad range of symptom levels. Of the 85 participants, 22 (26 %) had a BDI-II total of at least 14, 11 of at least 20 and 3 of at least 29; 10 had a PCL-5 total of at least 33 and 13 met the DSM-5 symptom criteria of the PCL-5; 23 had a SIAS total of at least 34 and 11 of at least 43; 5 were at or above the EAT-26 cut-off of 20; and 5 reported four or more adverse childhood experiences. Thirty-five participants had an OCI-12 total of at least 11, the cut-off of the OCI-12 validation (Abramovitch et al., 2021). The distributions of the BDI-II (skewness 1.24), PCL-5 (1.39), EAT-26 (1.80) and STICSA state (1.12) were right-skewed, and the IU total was symmetric (0.01). The totals of the anxiety, depression, obsessive-compulsive and posttraumatic scales correlated between .55 and .74 (Spearman), whereas the IU correlated more strongly with the OCI (.61) and the ERS (.56) than with the STICSA state total (.41).","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"SpZZszyymR"}],"key":"IbSPnOlOkj"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/tambi-website/_public/fig_totals_spearman-51a6c492517e0988d3b0be77d13236c2.png","alt":"Spearman correlations between the questionnaire totals (85 participants with behavioural data).","width":"70%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"e6mIZLbJaF","urlSource":"figures/fig_totals_spearman.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":51,"colu